.TH "ETR" "1" "" "etr 0.10.0" "User Commands"

.SH "NAME"
.PP
etr \- Eternal Terminal client

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBetr\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP] [\fIuser\fP@]\fIhost\fP [\fIcommand\fP [\fIargs\fP\.\.\.]]
.PP
\fBetr\fP \fB\-\-completions\fP \fISHELL\fP

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBetr\fP connects to a remote host and starts a persistent terminal session
that survives network interruptions\. Unlike SSH, the session keeps running
on the server when the connection drops, and \fBetr\fP reconnects transparently
when the network comes back\.
.PP
\fBetr\fP requires no pre\-running daemon on the server\. It bootstraps the
session by SSHing to the remote host, starting \fBetrs\fP(1) on the fly, and
then connecting to it over QUIC (TLS 1\.3)\. Certificate pinning is used in
place of a CA: the server certificate is transmitted over the authenticated
SSH channel and pinned for subsequent QUIC connections\.

.SH "OPTIONS"
\fB\fI[user@]host\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Remote host to connect to\. May include an optional \fIuser\fP prefix
(e\.g\. \fBalice@example\.com\fP)\. If omitted, help is printed\.
.RE
\fB\fIcommand\fP [\fIargs\fP\.\.\.]\fP
.RS
.PP
Optional remote command to execute instead of an interactive shell\.
All words after \fIhost\fP are joined with spaces and passed to \fBsh \-c\fP
on the remote host, so shell metacharacters work as expected\.
The command runs under a PTY, so full\-screen programs like \fBbtop\fP,
\fBvim\fP, and \fBdistrobox\fP work correctly\.
When the command exits the session ends\.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr host 'distrobox \-\- btop'
etr host ls \-la /tmp
etr host 'tail \-f /var/log/syslog | grep error'
.fi
.RE
.RE
\fB\fB\-s\fP, \fB\-\-ssh\-port\fP \fIPORT\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
SSH port to use for the initial bootstrap connection\. Defaults to 22,
or the value of \fBssh_port\fP in the config file\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-4\fP, \fB\-\-prefer\-ipv4\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Prefer IPv4 when the host resolves to both families\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-6\fP, \fB\-\-prefer\-ipv6\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Prefer IPv6 when the host resolves to both families\.
.PP
These are a \fBpreference, not a restriction\fP \-\- unlike \fBssh\fP(1)'s
\fB\-4\fP/\fB\-6\fP\. The requested family is tried first for the SSH bootstrap,
the QUIC session, and the targets of \fB\-R\fP forwards; the other family is
still used when the host has no address of the preferred one, or has one
the kernel cannot route to\. \fBetr\fP therefore never fails purely because
the requested family was unavailable, and the fallback is reported at
\fB\-v\fP\.
.PP
The preference is also sent to \fBetrs\fP(1), which applies it when
resolving the targets of \fB\-L\fP forwards on its own side\.
.PP
The two flags are mutually exclusive\. Config file equivalent:
\fBaddress_family\fP under \fB[client]\fP\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-v\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Increase verbosity\. May be repeated up to three times:
.PP
\fB\-v\fP \-\- connection lifecycle events
.PP
\fB\-vv\fP \-\- QUIC details and session ID
.PP
\fB\-vvv\fP \-\- per\-stream trace
.PP
When running interactively, verbose output is written to
\fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/etr/etr\.log\fP (default: \fB~/\.local/state/etr/etr\.log\fP)
to avoid corrupting the raw\-mode terminal display\. Before the session
enters raw mode, output goes to stderr\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-L\fP [\fIbind_address\fP:]\fIlocal_port\fP:\fIremote_host\fP:\fIremote_port\fP[/\fItcp\fP|/\fIudp\fP]\fP
.RS
.PP
Forward a local port to a remote destination, similar to \fBssh \-L\fP\.
The protocol defaults to TCP if not specified\. By default, the local listener
binds to both \fB127\.0\.0\.1\fP and \fB[::1]\fP loopback interfaces\.
.PP
If \fIbind_address\fP is specified as \fB*\fP, \fB0\.0\.0\.0\fP, or \fB::\fP, the listener binds
a single dual\-stack \fB[::]\fP socket, which accepts both IPv4 and IPv6 connections\.
Alternatively, it can bind to a specific interface IP\.
The \fB\-g\fP / \fB\-\-gateway\-ports\fP flag can also be used to bind all forwards to all interfaces\.
.PP
This option may be repeated to open multiple forwards concurrently alongside the PTY
session\.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\-L 8080:localhost:80
\-L *:3000:localhost:3000
\-L [::1]:8080:localhost:80
\-L 5353:8\.8\.8\.8:53/udp
\-L 5432:db\.internal:5432/tcp
.fi
.RE
.RE
\fB\fB\-R\fP [\fIbind_address\fP:]\fIremote_port\fP:\fIlocal_host\fP:\fIlocal_port\fP[/\fItcp\fP|/\fIudp\fP]\fP
.RS
.PP
Forward a remote port on the server to a local destination, similar to \fBssh \-R\fP\.
The protocol defaults to TCP if not specified\. By default, the remote listener
on the server binds to both \fB127\.0\.0\.1\fP and \fB[::1]\fP loopback interfaces\.
.PP
If \fIbind_address\fP is specified as \fB*\fP, \fB0\.0\.0\.0\fP, or \fB::\fP, the remote listener binds
a single dual\-stack \fB[::]\fP socket on the server\. Alternatively, it can bind to a specific
interface IP\.  The \fB\-g\fP / \fB\-\-gateway\-ports\fP flag also causes remote listeners to bind
to all interfaces on the server\.
.PP
This option may be repeated to open multiple reverse forwards concurrently alongside the PTY
session\.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\-R 8080:localhost:80
\-R 0\.0\.0\.0:3000:localhost:3000
\-R 5353:127\.0\.0\.1:53/udp
.fi
.RE
.RE
\fB\fB\-g\fP, \fB\-\-gateway\-ports\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Allow remote hosts to connect to forwarded ports\. Similar to \fBssh \-g\fP\.
Binds both local (\fB\-L\fP) and remote (\fB\-R\fP) forwarded ports to a single dual\-stack
\fB[::]\fP socket, which accepts both IPv4 and IPv6 connections on all interfaces,
instead of binding to loopback only\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-X\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Enable X11 forwarding\. Extracts the local X11 display cookie from the active display (via \fBxauth\fP) and securely forwards it to the server\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-Y\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Enable trusted X11 forwarding\. Treated identically to \fB\-X\fP\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-\-server\-path\fP \fIPATH\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Path to the \fBetrs\fP binary on the remote host\. Defaults to \fBetrs\fP
(relies on \fBPATH\fP), or the value of \fBserver_path\fP in the config file\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-\-log\-path\fP \fIPATH\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Path to the client log file\. Defaults to \fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/etr/etr\.log\fP\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-\-server\-log\-path\fP \fIPATH\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Path to the server log file on the remote host\. Defaults to \fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/etr/etrs\.log\fP\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-\-completions\fP \fISHELL\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Print shell completion script for the given shell and exit\. Supported
shells: \fBbash\fP, \fBzsh\fP, \fBfish\fP, \fBelvish\fP, \fBpowershell\fP, \fBnushell\fP\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Print help and exit\.
.RE
\fB\fB\-V\fP, \fB\-\-version\fP\fP
.RS
.PP
Print version and exit\.
.RE

.SH "CONFIGURATION"
.PP
\fBetr\fP reads a TOML config file from
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/etr/config\.toml\fP (default: \fB~/\.config/etr/config\.toml\fP)\.
All fields are optional\. CLI flags take precedence over config values\.
.RS
.PP
.nf
[client]
# Default SSH port (default: 22)
ssh_port = 22

# Path to etrs on remote hosts (default: "etrs", relies on PATH)
server_path = "/usr/local/bin/etrs"

# Which IP version to try first: "ipv4", "ipv6" or "auto" (default)
address_family = "auto"

# Default path to the client log file
log_path = "/tmp/client\.log"

# Default path to the server log file on the remote host
server_log_path = "/tmp/server\.log"

# Allow remote hosts to connect to local forwarded ports (default: false)
gateway_ports = true

# Default local port forwards
forward = ["8080:localhost:80", "*:3000:localhost:3000"]

# Default remote port forwards
reverse_forward = ["9090:localhost:90"]

# Enable X11 forwarding (default: false)
x11 = false

# Enable trusted X11 forwarding (default: false)
x11_trusted = false
.fi
.RE

.SH "FILES"
\fB\fB~/\.config/etr/config\.toml\fP\fP
.RS
Client configuration file (XDG_CONFIG_HOME honoured)\.
.RE
\fB\fB~/\.local/state/etr/etr\.log\fP\fP
.RS
Client verbose log file written during raw\-mode sessions
(XDG_STATE_HOME honoured)\.
.RE

.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
\fB\fBTERM\fP\fP
.RS
Passed to the remote shell via the bootstrap protocol\. Defaults to
\fBxterm\-256color\fP if unset\.
.RE
\fB\fBSHELL\fP\fP
.RS
Not used by \fBetr\fP itself; used by \fBetrs\fP(1) on the server to
determine which shell to launch\.
.RE
\fB\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP\fP
.RS
Base directory for the config file\. Defaults to \fB~/\.config\fP\.
.RE
\fB\fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fP\fP
.RS
Base directory for the log file\. Defaults to \fB~/\.local/state\fP\.
.RE

.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
Connect to a remote host:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr user@example\.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
Connect on a non\-standard SSH port:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr \-s 2222 user@example\.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
Connect with verbose logging:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr \-vv user@example\.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prefer IPv6 for a dual\-stack host (falls back to IPv4 if there is no
usable IPv6 address):
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr \-6 user@example\.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
Forward a local port to a remote database:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr \-L 5432:db\.internal:5432 user@example\.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
Forward a UDP port (DNS) through a jump host:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr \-L 5353:8\.8\.8\.8:53/udp user@jumphost
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reverse forward a remote port on the server to a local web server:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr \-R 8080:localhost:80 user@example\.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reverse forward allowing external connections to the server's port:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr \-R 0\.0\.0\.0:8080:localhost:80 user@example\.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a remote command under a PTY (session ends when the command exits):
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr user@example\.com 'distrobox \-\- btop'
etr user@example\.com ls \-la /tmp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate zsh completions:
.RS
.PP
.nf
etr \-\-completions zsh > ~/\.zfunc/_etr
.fi
.RE

.SH "ESCAPE SEQUENCES"
.PP
While a session is active, \fBetr\fP recognises SSH\-style escape sequences at
the start of a line (after a newline or at session start):
\fB\fB~\.\fP\fP
.RS
Force\-disconnect and exit\. Use this when the server is unreachable and
the normal reconnect loop is not making progress\.
.RE
\fB\fB~~\fP\fP
.RS
Send a literal `\f[CR]~\fP` to the remote shell\.
.RE

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
\fB\fB0\fP\fP
.RS
Clean disconnect\.
.RE
\fB\fBnon\-zero\fP\fP
.RS
Connection or session error\.
.RE

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBetrs\fP(1), \fBssh\fP(1), \fBssh_config\fP(5)

.SH "BUGS"
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
Session state is not persisted; re\-attaching from a different client
machine is not supported\.
.RE

